WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.280
He was the heart of perhaps the greatest television
drama of all time - but that hasn't translated

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.480
to big screen stardom.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:11.560
In a recent sitdown with Vulture, Aaron Paul
- star of the AMC TV series Breaking Bad and

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.170
its upcoming sequel film, El Camino: A Breaking
Bad Movie - offered a little insight as to

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.640
why he hasn't popped up more often in feature
films, and why he's not likely to in the future.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.040
Paul recalled that, in the wake of the wild
popularity of Breaking Bad and his subsequent

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.199
stardom, he was eager to parlay his success
into starring roles in other projects, whether

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.680
on the big screen or small - and he was in
no way hurting for opportunities.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:33.440
He confessed:

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.700
"I was so spoiled at the height of Breaking
Bad.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.340
I was being offered everything."

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:42.380
After Breaking Bad ended in 2013, he starred
in several smaller, independent features,

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.260
though they did little to raise his profile.

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:49.280
Paul's chance to make a splash in a big, expensive
blockbuster came his way in 2014, when he

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.760
appeared in the lead role in the video game
adaptation Need For Speed.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.060
Unfortunately, the flick failed to jump-start
Paul's movie career.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.560
It underperformed at the box office and was
savaged by critics, and his next big studio

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.650
effort - the Ridley Scott-helmed biblical
epic Exodus: Gods and Kings - didn't fare

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.220
much better.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.520
It simply seemed that Paul's acting style
- all barely contained emotion, with worlds

00:01:09.539 --> 00:01:13.969
of subtext plainly visible in his soulful,
expressive eyes - was much better suited for

00:01:13.969 --> 00:01:17.560
the small screen, and he also appeared to
thrive the most when his characters were given

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.540
the room to breathe and grow afforded by the
serial format.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.800
His next television effort, in the lead role
in the Hulu original series The Path, drew

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.899
critical accolades for his performance - but
the creative minds behind the series weren't

00:01:28.899 --> 00:01:33.079
quite as focused as those responsible for
Breaking Bad, and Hulu axed the show after

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.020
three seasons.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.460
Paul continued to surface in indie features
and smaller major studio productions, but

00:01:38.469 --> 00:01:42.600
he continually ran up against the same problem:
casting directors who seemed to think that

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:46.320
they were securing the services of Jesse Pinkman,
rather than Aaron Paul.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:51.679
Exhibit A: the 2016 Dwayne Johnson/Kevin Hart
comedy Central Intelligence, in which Paul's

00:01:51.679 --> 00:01:55.729
character makes frequent use of a certain
five-letter "B" word with which Pinkman was

00:01:55.729 --> 00:01:57.359
known to pepper every other sentence.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.180
"I'll give you three seconds to get your ass
out here.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:01.340
One…

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:02.560
Two...

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.920
"This is my own private domicile and I will
not be harassed…

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.340
b----!"

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:12.739
Eventually, the phone calls from the big studios
became fewer and further between, due to the

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:16.330
perception that Paul couldn't carry a major
Hollywood production on his own.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:17.140
The actor explained:

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.920
"You do one commercial film that's not the
success they think it's going to be, and you're

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:21.420
damned."

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.659
Fortunately, Paul had found plenty of space
to flex his creative muscles as the voice

00:02:25.659 --> 00:02:30.330
of the eternally cheerful yet deceptively
complex Todd Chavez on the Netflix animated

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:35.440
series BoJack Horseman, and roles in smaller
features - such as Zack Whedon's 2016 thriller

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.870
Come and Find Me and Jake Scott's 2018 drama
American Woman - have kept him busy.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.660
We'll be seeing him on the small screen early
and often in the months to come, starting

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:47.831
with a triumphant return to his signature
role in El Camino: A Breaking Bad Movie, an

00:02:47.831 --> 00:02:51.980
opportunity which presented itself directly
after his agent had discouraged him from taking

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:56.300
a role in a feature that went on to become,
quote, "a monster, monster hit."

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.300
Paul declined to name said feature.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:02:59.540
Paul said of that experience:

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.460
"There were some big thorns in my side that
I had to let go.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.450
My wife knew all of this was going on, so
when I told her what [Breaking Bad creator]

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.120
Vince [Gilligan] wanted to do, she threw her
arms around me.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.040
She knew it was an opportunity for me to spread
my wings again."

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.980
Breaking Bad fans absolutely cannot wait to
see Paul saddle up as Jesse Pinkman one more

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:20.680
time, and while it's almost certainly the
last we'll ever see of Pinkman, we're going

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.999
to be seeing plenty of Paul.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.359
He can currently be seen in one of those small
indie thrillers, The Parts You Lose, as a

00:03:26.359 --> 00:03:30.700
violent fugitive who forges an unlikely friendship
with a deaf youngster - but he seems most

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:35.190
excited about his involvement in the upcoming
third season of HBO's Westworld, in which

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.240
he'll play a role that's shrouded in secrecy.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.220
As far as big-budget features are concerned,
however, Paul appears to have had his fill

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:41.860
of that world.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:42.720
He said:

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.000
"I can't live with that pressure on me, nor
will I. I'm happy."

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.760
We can only hope that Pinkman will be able
to say the same at the conclusion of his story.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:53.560
El Camino: A Breaking Bad Movie premieres
on Netflix on October 11.

